To make this solution more efficient for handling large amounts of text, we can use memory-mapped files (mmaps) instead of loading the entire text into memory at once. Here's an updated implementation:

```matlab
function uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1File, text2File)
    % Read text1 and text2 into memory-mapped files
    mmapOptions = 'r';
    mmapSize = max(32 * 1024 * 1024, stat(text1File).size); % Allocate at least 32 MB
    mmapText1 = mmread(text1File, mmapOptions, mmapSize);
    mmapText2 = mmread(text2File, mmapOptions, mmapSize);

    % Convert text to lowercase and split into words using mmapped memory
    mmappedWords1 = lower(strsplit(mmapText1));
    mmappedWords2 = lower(strsplit(mmapText2));

    % Find unique words without loading them into memory
    uniqueWords = setdiff(mmappedWords1(:), mmappedWords2(:));

    % Close the memory-mapped files
    mmclose(mmapText1);
    mmclose(mmapText2);
end

% Usage example
text1File = 'Text1.txt';
text2File = 'Text2.txt';
uniqueWords = findUniqueWords(text1File, text2File);
disp(uniqueWords);
```

This implementation uses memory-mapped files to read the text data into memory without actually loading it into RAM. The `mmread()` function reads the data lazily as needed, which reduces the amount of memory used during processing. By allocating at least 32 MB for the memory mapping, we ensure that the entire file can be read into memory without having to allocate more than necessary. Once we have found the unique words, we close the memory-mapped files using `mmclose()`. This approach allows us to process very large text files without running out of memory or disk I/O errors due to excessive file input/output.

