```typescript
import { Injectable, NestMiddleware } from '@nestjs/common';
import { Observable } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable()
export class DevModeMiddleware implements NestMiddleware {
  resolve(): Function {
    return (req: any, res: any, next: () => any) => {
      if (process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development') {
        return next();
      } else {
        return false;
      }
    };
  }
}

// Example usage in a controller or route
@UseGuards(AuthGuard)
@UsePipes(ValidationPipe)
@UseInterceptors(ResponseTimeInterceptor)
@UseFilters(AllExceptionsFilter)
@UseMiddleware(new DevModeMiddleware())
export class MyController {}
```

In this example, we're creating a middleware called `DevModeMiddleware` that checks the value of the `NODE_ENV` environment variable. If it's set to "development", then the request is passed along to the next middleware or controller handler using the `next()` function. If `NODE_ENV` is not "development", then the middleware returns `false`, effectively stopping further execution of middleware and controller handlers.

To use this middleware, you can add it to your routes or controllers using the `@UseMiddleware()` decorator, passing an instance of the middleware as the argument. This will ensure that the middleware is executed before any other middleware or controller handlers for requests where `NODE_ENV` is "development". For requests where `NODE_ENV` is not "development", the middleware will immediately return `false`, preventing any further middleware or controller execution.

This middleware is designed to help with development workflows by allowing certain functionality or debugging features to only be available in development mode, while still providing production-ready behavior for all other environments. It can also be useful for testing and quality assurance purposes, where you may want to simulate specific environments without modifying the codebase itself.

